# Private nested backend global-version contract

The real first foundation run proved Intel Mesa GL and complete DMA-BUF feedback,
then the installed Aquamarine0.15 client sent wl_compositor bind version6 to a
Weston global advertised as version5. The server rejected the request; nested
Hyprland aborted before any producer, input fixture or plugin. Main15 state
checks and complete private cleanup passed. Preserve that attempt unchanged.

For each recognized registry global, choose the lesser of advertised and client
supported version. Bind only if that common version meets every request the
backend uses. The five policies are seat9/min5 (release), xdg_wm_base6/min1,
wl_compositor6/min4 (damage_buffer), wl_shm1/min1, linux_dmabuf4/min4
(get_default_feedback). An insufficient version must be logged and left absent;
the existing missing-protocol refusal then ends startup cleanly. Unknown globals
produce no binding. Never request more than the advertised version or silently
claim an unavailable protocol feature. No change to global server advertisement,
fake metadata, system library or main compositor is allowed.

Build only a private compatible Aquamarine library from the exact upstream
0.15.0 source snapshot, retaining original and changed files/diff. Public headers
must match installed headers, and the exported dynamic-symbol API must remain
compatible. The host's future nested child may use this reviewed private library;
Weston and the test renderer keep their existing reviewed libraries. Actual
process maps must prove which binary the nested compositor loaded.

The Quint state model checks binding/refusal contracts across supported and
insufficient versions. Actual C++ consumer tests exercise boundaries. A new
private foundation run must then prove server/client compatibility, real Intel
GL/DMA-BUF, nested output, exact library mapping and full normal cleanup. This
private compatibility repair grants no cadence or window-feature acceptance.
